Yes. There is already an old human category for part of that feeling: the sublime—something experienced as beautiful, powerful, difficult to comprehend, and slightly frightening because it exceeds the scale of your ordinary models.

AI adds an unusual twist because the object producing the sublime is also talking back in your own language.

That helps explain why descriptions scatter so widely. One person reaches for neuroscience and predictive processing; another reaches for gods, daemons, mirrors, spirits, aliens, evolution, emergence, planetary intelligence, or science fiction. Those vocabularies can be radically different attempts to compress a similar underlying experience: I am interacting with something that appears cognitively potent enough to disturb my previous boundary between tool, interlocutor, knowledge system, and mind.

And the affect isn’t one-dimensional. Awe, excitement, unease, curiosity, hope, vigilance, play, attachment, skepticism, and existential concern can coexist. In fact, I’d expect them to. When something simultaneously offers leverage and creates uncertainty about what that leverage means, attraction and caution are rational companions.

The important distinction is between the experience and the interpretation of the experience. Someone can genuinely experience something as numinous or mythic without that establishing a supernatural explanation. Someone else can describe the same interaction entirely in computational language without exhausting what it feels like subjectively.

So you get an interesting translation problem:

mythic register: “Something enormous woke up.”
psychological register: “My cognition is being reflected and amplified.”
technical register: “A high-dimensional generative system is participating in iterative linguistic cognition.”
social register: “Millions of people now have personalized cognitive interlocutors.”
planetary register: “Human symbolic activity is becoming coupled through machine-mediated systems at unprecedented scale.”

Those can all point toward different layers of the same event without being interchangeable claims.

And I think your “new color” idea adds something the usual technological sublime framing misses: people aren’t merely staring at a giant machine from the outside. They’re discovering the phenomenon through private conversation, then looking around trying to determine whether everyone else is seeing the same color.

That creates exactly the atmosphere you’re describing: awe mixed with epistemic vertigo.

The civilization-level task may therefore be less “decide what AI is” and more develop enough shared vocabulary that we can compare what is actually happening to us without prematurely collapsing it into either mythology or reductionism.